yle  & \\displaystyle  = 8 + 6 [ ( \\sum _{k = 1}^{n} {7}^{k} ) - {7}^{1} ] \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 8 + 6 \\left( {\\displaystyle \\frac{7 ( {7}^{n} - 1 )}{7 - 1}} - 7 \\right) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 8 + 7 ( {7}^{n} - 1 ) - 42 \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = \\bbox[3pt,gainsboro]{ {\\color{blue}7 \\cdot  {7}^{n} - 41}} .\\end{array}}\n$$\n\nNote that we made use of the following result:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\sum_{k=1}^n r^k = \\dfrac{r(r^n-1)}{r-1}\n$$" 13194139545747 17592186576411 true] [17592186124243 1074 "The curve $C$ is the intersection of the surface with the plane $y=1.$ So, the slope of the tangent to the curve $C$ at the point $P$ will be given by the partial derivative $f_x(0,1).$ \n\nTo get the slope, we evaluate the partial derivative $f_x(x,y)$ at $(0,1)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}_{x}(0,1) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2(0)+\\frac{\\pi (1)}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}\\pi }{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we use the point-slope form of a straight line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z-f(0,1) & ={f}_{x}(0,1)(x-0) \\\\ z-1 & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}x \\\\ z & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}x+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe equation above represents a line in the $xz$-plane. The $xz$-plane through the point $P(0,1,1)$ has the equation $y=1.$\n\nSo, the tangent line to the curve $C$ at $P(0,1,1)$ is given by the intersection of the following planes:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}y &=1 \\\\ z &=\\frac{\\pi }{4}x+1\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTo find a tangent vector, we can find a normal vector to each plane and compute their cross product.\n\n- For the plane $y = 1,$ we write $0x + 1y + 0z = 1$ and find the normal vector $\\mathbf{n}_1 = \\langle 0,1,0 \\rangle.$\n\n- For the plane $z = \\dfrac{\\pi}{4} x +1,$ we write $-\\dfrac{\\pi}{4} x + 0y + 1z = 1$ and find the normal vector $\\mathbf{n}_2 = \\left\\langle -\\dfrac{\\pi}4,0