24908 1074 "If the set is linearly dependent, then there exist ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ not both zero such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)=0.\n$$\n\nOtherwise, if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0,$ the set is linearly independent.\n\nSubstituting the given expressions for ${p}_{1}(t)$ and ${p}_{2}(t)$ into the above equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(2t-4{t}^{2})+{x}_{2}(3t-6{t}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ (2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2})t+(-4{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2}){t}^{2} & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation is true for every $t$ if and only if \n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2} &=0 \\\\ -4{x}_{1}-6{x}_{2} &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, we consider the corresponding augmented matrix and reduce it to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -6 & 0\\end{array}\\right] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\left[\\begin{array}{cc|c}2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{array}\\right].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe pivot column is the $1$st one. Hence, ${x}_{2}$ is a free variable. From the $1$st equation, we get\n\n$$\n2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2}=0\\,⟹\\,{x}_{1}=-\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}{x}_{1} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2} \\\\ {x}_{2}\\end{bmatrix},\\hspace{2em}{x}_{2}\\in \\mathbb{R}.\n$$\n\nFinally, for any ${x}_{2}\\ne 0,$ we obtain \n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{2}}{{x}_{1}}=\\frac{{x}_{2}}{(-\\frac{3}{2}{x}_{2})}=-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$" 13194139545747 17592186576411 false] [17592186124908 1074 "If the set is linearly dependent, then there exist ${x}_{1},{x}_{2}$ not both zero such that \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}{p}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}{p}_{2}(t)=0.\n$$\n\nOtherwise, if ${x}_{1}={x}_{2}=0,$ the set is linearly independent.\n\nSubstituting the given expressions for ${p}_{1}(t)$ and ${p}_{2}(t)$ into the above equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(2t-4{t}^{2})+{x}_{2}(3t-6{t}^{2}) & =0 \\\\ (2{x}_{1}+3{x}_{2})t+(-4{x}