ve of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{t}{2}\\right)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApplying the chain rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))] & =\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{t}{2}\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j})\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\mathbf{j}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}t\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{t}{2}\\operatorname{cos}t\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}t+t\\operatorname{cos}t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating the above at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right))] & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\operatorname{cos}\\left(\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1+\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\cdot 0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545747 17592186576411 false] [17592186125246 1074 "The chain rule in vector form states that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}[f(\\mathbf{r}(t))]=\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t))\\cdot {\\mathbf{r}}^{'}(t).\n$$\n\nIn addition, we've been given that\n\n$$\n\\mathrm{∇}f(x,y)=\\operatorname{sin}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+x\\operatorname{cos}y\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we substitute $x=\\frac{t}{2}$ and $y=t$ into $∇f(x,y)$ to find $\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{∇}f(\\mathbf{r}(t)) & =\\operatorname{sin}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{i}+\\frac{t}{2}\\operatorname{cos}t\\hspace{0.167em}\\mathbf{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we calculate the derivative of $\\mathbf{r}(t),$ which is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\mathbf{r