name{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nFrom the above equations, we can express $\\rho $, $\\theta $, and $ϕ$ in terms of $x$, $y$, and $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{y}{x},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\\frac{z}{\\rho }\n$$\n\nFirst, we find $\\rho :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\rho  & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{1}^{2}+(-1)^{2}+(\\sqrt{2})^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1+2} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, $(x,y)=(1,-1)$ lies in the fourth quadrant. So to find $\\theta ,$ we first need to find the reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{y}{x}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡|\\frac{-1}{1}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we subtract the reference angle from $2\\pi $ to get $\\theta :$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =2\\pi -{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & =2\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, for $ϕ$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}ϕ & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(\\frac{z}{\\rho }\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡\\left(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $A$ are $(2,\\frac{7\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$" 13194139545747 17592186576411 false] [17592186129866 1074 "If $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ)$ are the spherical coordinates of a point, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found by using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,y=\\rho \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡ϕ,\\,z=\\rho \\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ\n$$\n\nFrom the above equations, we can express $\\rho $, $\\theta $, and $ϕ$ in terms of $x$, $y$, and $z$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\rho =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}+{z}^{2}},\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡\\theta =\\frac{y}{x},\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡ϕ=\