411 true] [17592186129869 1074 "We write the position of a point in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where $\\rho \\ge 0,$ $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ and $0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi .$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following:\n\n- The distance from the origin to the point $D$ equals $2$ units.\n\n- The half-plane that contains the point $D$ and whose boundary is the $z$-axis forms an angle of \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\n radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and the point $D$ forms an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ radians with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $D$ are $(2,\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{4}).$" 13194139545747 17592186576411 false] [17592186129869 1074 "We write the position of a point in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,ϕ),$ where $\\rho \\ge 0,$ $0\\le \\theta <2\\pi ,$ and $0\\le ϕ\\le \\pi .$\n\nAccording to the diagram, we have the following:\n\n- The distance from the origin to the point $D$ equals $2$ units.\n\n- The half-plane that contains the point $D$ and whose boundary is the $z$-axis forms an angle of \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi }{2}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}\n$$\n\n radians with the positive $x$-axis.\n\n- The line that passes through the origin and the point $D$ forms an angle of $\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ radians with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nTherefore, the spherical coordinates of $D$ are $\\left(2,\\frac{\\pi }{3},\\frac{\\pi }{4}\\right).$" 13194139545747 17592186576411 true] [17592186129878 1074 "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=x-y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $x+y=u$ and $x-y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+y)^{4}-x+y=0\\,⟹\\,(x+y)^{4}-(x-y)=0\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y &=u \\\\ x-y &=v\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{u-v}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this value into the equation of the given line yields:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=0\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{u-v}{2}=0\\,⟹\\,v=u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &={u}^{4} \\\\ v &=u\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{3}(u-1) &=0 \\\\ v &=u.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the intersection points are $(0,0)$ and $(1,1).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,{u}^{4}\\le v\\le u\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/1e/1ed905e319e689d3fc7955a30f58e0f3ae2e7a751c27b80201d2b43f58b4206c.png)\n\n \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(-2)^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{{u}^{4}}^{u}\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[v]_{{u}^{4}}^{u}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(u-{u}^{4})du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{u}^{5}}{5}]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{3}{10}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545747 17592186576411 false] [17592186129878 1074 "The area of the required region is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{T}:\\begin{bmatrix}u \\\\ v\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}x(u,v) \\\\ y(u,v)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uv$-plane to our region $D$ in the $xy$-plane.\n\nTo compute the required area, we will use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}dxdy={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\text{ }dudv\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$  \n\nNote that the changes of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=x-y\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:\\begin{bmatrix}x \\\\ y\\end{bmatrix}\\to \\begin{bmatrix}u(x,y) \\\\ v(x,y)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTo compute the required area, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $D$ under the action of ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}.$ \n\nSubstituting $x+y=u$ and $x-y=v$ into the equation of the first curve, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+y)^{4}-x+y=0\\,⟹\\,(x+y)^{4}-(x-y)=0\\,⟹\\,v={u}^{4},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x+y &=u \\\\ x-y &=v\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,y=\\frac{u-v}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this value into the equation of the given line yields:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=0\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{u-v}{2}=0\\,⟹\\,v=u\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo find the intersections of the curves in the $uv$-plane, we solve the following system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}v &={u}^{4} \\\\ v &=u\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{u}^{3}(u-1) &=0 \\\\ v &=u.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, the intersection points are $(0,0)$ and $(1,1).$\n\nTherefore, our domain in the $uv$-plane has the type I representation\n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(u,v)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,{u}^{4}\\le v\\le u\\}.\n$$\n\n![](images/1e/1ed905e319e689d3fc7955a30f58e0f3ae2e7a751c27b80201d2b43f58b4206c.png)\n\n \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${\\mathbf{T}}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)} & =\\begin{vmatrix}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y}\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{vmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}={\\left(\\frac{∂(u,v)}{∂(x,y)}\\right)}^{-1}=(-2)^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$  In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Compute the required area.\n\nWe calculate the required area by performing the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\text{d}x\\text{d}y & ={∬}_{Δ}|\\frac{∂(x,y)}{∂(u,v)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & ={∬}_{Δ}|-\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{{u}^{4}}^{u}\\,dv]du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[v]_{{u}^{4}}^{u}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}(u-{u}^{4})du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\left[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}-\\frac{{u}^{5}}{5}\\right]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{1}{5}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\left(\\frac{3}{10}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{20}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545747 17592186576411 true] [17592186129884 1071 "Given that the discrete random variable $Y$ has the cumulative distribution function $F(y)$ shown in the table below, compute $P(\\frac{1}{2}\\le Y\\le \\frac{3}{2}).$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{3}{2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$F(y)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n" 13194139545747 17592186576411 false] [17592186129884 1071 "Given that the discrete random variable $Y$ has the cumulative distribution function $F(y)$ shown in the table below, compute $P\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\le Y\\le \\frac{3}{2}\\right).$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$y$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{1}{2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\frac{3}{2}$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$F(y)$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.4$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.8$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1.0$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n" 13194139545747 17592186576411 true] [17592186129884 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $Y$ is a function $F(y)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(y)=P(Y\\le y).\n$$\n\nTo compute the probability of a random variable $Y$ over an interval, we can use the rule\n\n$$\nP(a<Y\\le b)=F(b)-F(a).\n$$\n\nHere, we want to compute $P(\\frac{1}{2}\\le Y\\le \\frac{3}{2}).$ However, this interval is stated in the form $a\\le Y\\le b,$ not $a<Y\\le b.$\n\nSo first, we must convert the interval $\\frac{1}{2}\\le Y\\le \\frac{3}{2}$ to the form $a<Y\\le b.$ Looking at the given possible values of $Y,$ we see that $\\frac{1}{2}\\le Y$ is equivalent to $0<Y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\frac{1}{2}\\le Y\\le \\frac{3}{2}) & =P(0<Y\\le \\frac{3}{2}) \\\\  & =F\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)-F(0) \\\\  & =0.8-0.1 \\\\  & =0.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545747 17592186576411 false] [17592186129884 1074 "The cumulative distribution function of a discrete random variable $Y$ is a function $F(y)$ such that\n\n$$\nF(y)=P(Y\\le y).\n$$\n\nTo compute the probability of a random variable $Y$ over an interval, we can use the rule\n\n$$\nP(a<Y\\le b)=F(b)-F(a).\n$$\n\nHere, we want to compute $P\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\le Y\\le \\frac{3}{2}\\right).$ However, this interval is stated in the form $a\\le Y\\le b,$ not $a<Y\\le b.$\n\nSo first, we must convert the interval $\\frac{1}{2}\\le Y\\le \\frac{3}{2}$ to the form $a<Y\\le b.$ Looking at the given possible values of $Y,$ we see that $\\frac{1}{2}\\le Y$ is equivalent to $0<Y.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P\\left(\\frac{1}{2}\\le Y\\le \\frac{3}{2}\\right) & =P\\left(0<Y\\le \\frac{3}{2}\\right) \\\\  & =F\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)-F(0) \\\\  & =0.8-0.1 \\\\  & =0.7.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545747 17592186576411 true] [17592186129887 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\frac{1}{2}$ centered at \n$(0,-\\frac{1}{2}).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $x=\\sqrt{-{y}^{2}-y}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt{-{y}^{2}-y} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-{y}^{2}-y \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =-y \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta ) & =-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 0\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta [\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}]_{0}^{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ((-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{4}-{0}^{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\sin }^{4}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{[\\frac{{\\sin }^{5}⁡\\theta }{5}]}_{-\\pi /2}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}({\\sin }^{5}⁡0-{\\sin }^{5}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545747 17592186576411 false] [17592186129887 1074 "We will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that the region $D$ represents a portion of the disk of radius $\\frac{1}{2}$ centered at \n$\\left(0,-\\frac{1}{2}\\right).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $x=\\sqrt{-{y}^{2}-y}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt{-{y}^{2}-y} \\\\ {x}^{2} & =-{y}^{2}-y \\\\ {x}^{2}+{y}^{2} & =-y \\\\ (r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2} & =-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}({\\cos }^{2}⁡\\theta +{\\sin }^{2}⁡\\theta ) & =-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2} & =-r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\ {r}^{2}+r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r+\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire semicircle, we must have $r=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nTherefore, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le 0\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )\\sqrt{(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\int }_{0}^{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{3}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\left[\\frac{{r}^{4}}{4}\\right]_{0}^{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ((-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{4}-{0}^{4})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{0}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta {\\sin }^{4}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\left[\\frac{{\\sin }^{5}⁡\\theta }{5}\\right]}_{-\\pi /2}^{0} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}\\left({\\sin }^{5}⁡0-{\\sin }^{5}⁡\\left(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{20}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545747 17592186576411 true] [17592186130343 1064 "To check that a polynomial division result is correct, we can multiply both sides of the equation by the divisor and ensure that both sides are equal.\n\nFor example, let's check that the following polynomial division result is correct.\n\n$$\n\\frac{2x^2+8x - 5}{x+5}=2x-2 +\\dfrac{5}{x+5}\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply the above by $(x+5)$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)\\times \\left(\\frac{2{x}^{2}+8x-5}{x+5}\\right) & =(x+5)\\times (2x-2+\\frac{5}{x+5}) \\\\ \\frac{(2{x}^{2}+8x-5)(x+5)}{x+5} & =(2x-2)(x+5)+\\frac{5(x+5)}{x+5} \\\\ \\frac{(2{x}^{2}+8x-5)\\cancel{(x+5)}}{\\cancel{x+5}} & =(2x-2)(x+5)+\\frac{5\\cancel{(x+5)}}{\\cancel{x+5}} \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+8x-5 & =(2x-2)(x+5)+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we expand the parentheses on the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+8x-5 & =(2x-2)(x+5)+5 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+8x-5 & =2{x}^{2}+10x-2x-10+5 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+8x-5 & =2{x}^{2}+8x-5\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a true statement. Therefore, the result is indeed correct." 13194139545747 17592186576411 false] [17592186130343 1064 "To check that a polynomial division result is correct, we can multiply both sides of the equation by the divisor and ensure that both sides are equal.\n\nFor example, let's check that the following polynomial division result is correct.\n\n$$\n\\frac{2x^2+8x - 5}{x+5}=2x-2 +\\dfrac{5}{x+5}\n$$\n\nFirst, we multiply the above by $(x+5)$ and simplify:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)\\times \\left(\\frac{2{x}^{2}+8x-5}{x+5}\\right) & =(x+5)\\times \\left(2x-2+\\frac{5}{x+5}\\right) \\\\ \\frac{(2{x}^{2}+8x-5)(x+5)}{x+5} & =(2x-2)(x+5)+\\frac{5(x+5)}{x+5} \\\\ \\frac{(2{x}^{2}+8x-5)\\cancel{(x+5)}}{\\cancel{x+5}} & =(2x-2)(x+5)+\\frac{5\\cancel{(x+5)}}{\\cancel{x+5}} \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+8x-5 & =(2x-2)(x+5)+5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we expand the parentheses on the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}+8x-5 & =(2x-2)(x+5)+5 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+8x-5 & =2{x}^{2}+10x-2x-10+5 \\\\ 2{x}^{2}+8x-5 & =2{x}^{2}+8x-5\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a true statement. Therefore, the result is indeed correct." 13194139545747 17592186576411 true] [17592186130381 1064 "A **repeated integral**, also known as an **iterated integral,** is an integral in which we carry out repeated integration with respect to different variables. An example of a repeated integral is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int_0^1\\int_0^1 ye^{xy}\\,\\textrm d x\\,\\textrm d y\n$$\n\nTo evaluate this integral, we first integrate with respect to the inner variable $x,$ and then with respect to the outer variable $y$. We can write this more explicitly as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}y{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now carry out the integration. First, we integrate with respect to $x,$ *treating $y$ as a constant*. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}y{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[\\frac{y{e}^{xy}}{y}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{e}^{xy}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{y}-{e}^{0}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{y}-1\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{y}-1\\,\\text{d}y & ={[{e}^{y}-y]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[e-1]-[1-0] \\\\  & =e-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int_0^1\\int_0^1 ye^{xy}\\,\\textrm d x\\,\\textrm d y = e-2.\n$$" 13194139545747 17592186576411 false] [17592186130381 1064 "A **repeated integral**, also known as an **iterated integral,** is an integral in which we carry out repeated integration with respect to different variables. An example of a repeated integral is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\int_0^1\\int_0^1 ye^{xy}\\,\\textrm d x\\,\\textrm d y\n$$\n\nTo evaluate this integral, we first integrate with respect to the inner variable $x,$ and then with respect to the outer variable $y$. We can write this more explicitly as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}y{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now carry out the integration. First, we integrate with respect to $x,$ *treating $y$ as a constant*. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}[{\\int }_{0}^{1}y{e}^{xy}\\,\\text{d}x]\\,\\text{d}y & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\left[\\frac{y{e}^{xy}}{y}\\right]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{[{e}^{xy}]}_{0}^{1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{y}-{e}^{0}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{y}-1\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we integrate with respect to $y\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{e}^{y}-1\\,\\text{d}y & ={[{e}^{y}-y]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =[e-1]-[1-0] \\\\  & =e-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that\n\n$$\n\\int_0^1\\int_0^1 ye^{xy}\\,\\textrm d x\\,\\textrm d y = e-2.\n$$" 13194139545747 17592186576411 true] [17592186130382 1064 "Earlier, we claimed that function $f(x)$ is differentiable at $x_0$ *if the function admits a unique tangent line* at $x_0.$ Let's now justify this claim.\n\nRecall that a function $f(x)$ is differentiable at $x=x_0$ with derivative $f'(x_0)$ if and only if the following limit exists:\n\n$$\nf'(x_0) = \\lim_{x\\to x_0}\\dfrac {f(x) - f(x_0)} {x-x_0}\n$$\n\nWe can rewrite this limit as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {x}_{0}}\\left(\\frac{f(x)-f({x}_{0})}{x-{x}_{0}}\\right)-{f}^{′}({x}_{0}) & =0 \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to {x}_{0}}(\\frac{f(x)-f({x}_{0})}{x-{x}_{0}}-{f}^{′}({x}_{0})) & =0 \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to {x}_{0}}\\frac{f(x)-f({x}_{0})-(x-{x}_{0}){f}^{′}({x}_{0})}{x-{x}_{0}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for the above limit to hold true, the numerator must approach zero. So, near $x_0,$ we must have\n\n$$\nf(x) - f(x_0) - (x-x_0)f'(x_0) \\approx 0,\n$$\n\nwhich can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nf(x) - f(x_0) \\approx (x-x_0)f'(x_0).\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following equation of the tangent line at $x_0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\ny -y_0 = (x-x_0)\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d x}(x_0)\n$$\n\nFrom here, we can say that a function $f(x)$ is differentiable at $x_0$ *if the function admits a unique tangent line* at $x_0.$\n\nWe'll now use a similar idea to define the differentiability of a multivariable function at a point $P$ in terms of tangent planes." 13194139545747 17592186576411 false] [17592186130382 1064 "Earlier, we claimed that function $f(x)$ is differentiable at $x_0$ *if the function admits a unique tangent line* at $x_0.$ Let's now justify this claim.\n\nRecall that a function $f(x)$ is differentiable at $x=x_0$ with derivative $f'(x_0)$ if and only if the following limit exists:\n\n$$\nf'(x_0) = \\lim_{x\\to x_0}\\dfrac {f(x) - f(x_0)} {x-x_0}\n$$\n\nWe can rewrite this limit as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\lim_{x\\to {x}_{0}}\\left(\\frac{f(x)-f({x}_{0})}{x-{x}_{0}}\\right)-{f}^{′}({x}_{0}) & =0 \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to {x}_{0}}\\left(\\frac{f(x)-f({x}_{0})}{x-{x}_{0}}-{f}^{′}({x}_{0})\\right) & =0 \\\\ \\lim_{x\\to {x}_{0}}\\frac{f(x)-f({x}_{0})-(x-{x}_{0}){f}^{′}({x}_{0})}{x-{x}_{0}} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for the above limit to hold true, the numerator must approach zero. So, near $x_0,$ we must have\n\n$$\nf(x) - f(x_0) - (x-x_0)f'(x_0) \\approx 0,\n$$\n\nwhich can be rewritten as\n\n$$\nf(x) - f(x_0) \\approx (x-x_0)f'(x_0).\n$$\n\nThis corresponds to the following equation of the tangent line at $x_0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\ny -y_0 = (x-x_0)\\dfrac{\\textrm d f}{\\textrm d x}(x_0)\n$$\n\nFrom here, we can say that a function $f(x)$ is differentiable at $x_0$ *if the function admits a unique tangent line* at $x_0.$\n\nWe'll now use a similar idea to define the differentiability of a multivariable function at a point $P$ in terms of tangent planes." 13194139545747 17592186576411 true] [17592186130428 1064 "Consider the following recurrence relation:\n\n$$\na_{n} - 3a_{n-1} + 2a_{n-2} = 2^n\n$$\n\nIt's easy to show that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\na_{n}^{(h)} = A + B\\cdot 2^n\n$$\n\nWhen it comes to selecting a form for a particular solution, we *cannot* pick\n\n$$\na_n^{(p)} = \\alpha \\cdot 2^n\n$$\n\nThis solution is already associated with the complementary solution. Therefore, the left-hand side of the recurrence relation evaluates to zero when this is form substituted into it.\n\nIndeed, we can check that if\n\n$$\na_n = \\alpha \\cdot 2^n\n$$\n\n, then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\na_{n} - 3a_{n - 1} + 2a_{n - 2} &= α \\cdot 2^{n} - 3α \\cdot 2^{n - 1} + 2α \\cdot 2^{n - 2} \\\\\n&= α \\cdot 2^{n} - \\frac{3}{2}α \\cdot