in{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dx+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y) = 2\\sin{y}.$" 13194139545747 17592186576411 false] [17592186133698 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states that\n\n$$\n\\oint\\limits_{C} P\\,\\mathrm{d}x + Q\\,\\mathrm{d}y \n= \\iint\\limits_D \\left(\\frac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x} - \\frac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm d A.\n$$\n\nThe region $D$ and the curve $C$ for this case are shown below.\n\n![](images/a7/a78a98750e3203760d27daaebd42852198538935e2c3777a0b8a8c85d1bb0901.png)\n\nWe have that $P(x,y)= \\cos{y}$ and $Q(x,y)= x\\sin{y}.$ So,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂P}{∂y}=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y,\\,\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying Green's theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{C}\\operatorname{cos}⁡y\\,dx+x\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,dy & ={∬}_{D}\\left(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y}\\right)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡y-(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡y)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡y\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $f(x,y) = 2\\sin{y}.$" 13194139545747 17592186576411 true] [17592186133699 1074 "Let $C$ be a positively oriented, piecewise-smooth, simple closed curve in the plane, and let $D$ be the region bounded by $C.$ If $P$ and $Q$ have continuous first partial derivatives on an open region containing $D$, then Green's theorem states tha