llustrate how this is done, let's consider the vector field $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j,$ and the line integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_{\\partial D} \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $D$ is the annular region below.\n\n![](images/57/57c22c3d7a92ce4a1a1a798f7e6a861ded2e9a6be4d8d98c36869fe29e2783d0.png)\n\nNote that $\\partial D= C_1\\cup C_2$ is positively oriented. \n\nNow, imagine splitting $D$ into two regions $D_1$ and $D_2$ by means of *cuts* along the segments $[-4, -2]$ and $[2,4]$ on the $x$-axis, as shown below \n\n![](images/e7/e7f0d41c3fb909073cdaf4ea23f4ab617032e0d24bdc8d9b9e3724e7d56abe3b.png)\n\nNotice that $D_1$ and $D_2$ are positively oriented type I regions. In addition, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{C}_{1} & ={C}_{3}\\cup {C}_{8} \\\\ {C}_{2} & ={C}_{5}\\cup {C}_{10} \\\\ ∂{D}_{1} & ={C}_{3}\\cup {C}_{4}\\cup {C}_{5}\\cup {C}_{6}, \\\\ ∂{D}_{2} & ={C}_{8}\\cup (-{C}_{6})\\cup {C}_{10}\\cup (-{C}_{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now write the circulation of $\\mathbf F$ over $\\partial D$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∮}_{∂D}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}\\cup \\hspace{0.167em}{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & ={∮}_{{C}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{∮}_{{C}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{\\int }_{{C}_{8}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{\\int }_{{C}_{5}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{\\int }_{{C}_{10}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & +\\underbrace{{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{\\int }_{-{C}_{4}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{\\int }_{{C}_{6}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{∮}_{-{C}_{6}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}}_{0} \\\\  & ={\\int }_{{C}_{3}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{\\int }_{{C}_{4}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{\\int }_{{C}_{5}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{\\int }_{{C}_{6}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & +{\\int }_{{C}_{8}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{\\int }_{-{C}_{6}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{\\int }_{{C}_{10}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{∮}_{-{C}_{4}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & ={∮}_{∂{D}_{1}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r}+{∮}_{∂{D}_{2}}\\mathbf{F}\\cdot \\text{d}\\mathbf{r} \\\\  & ={∮}_{∂{D}_{1}}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y+{∮}_{∂{D}_{2}}P\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}x+Q\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{{D}_{1}}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A+{∬}_{{D}_{2}}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂Q}{∂x}-\\frac{∂P}{∂y})\\hspace{0.167em}\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo summarize,\n\n$$\n\\oint \\limits_{\\partial D}\\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r = \\iint_\\limits{D}\\left(\\dfrac{\\partial Q}{\\partial x}-\\dfrac{\\partial P}{\\partial y}\\right)\\,\\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich is just Green's theorem for the region $D.$" 13194139545747 17592186576411 false] [17592186135140 1064 "When proving Green's theorem, we typically start by assuming that $D$ is a so-called **simple region** (that is, a region that is both type I and type II). Then, Green's theorem is *extended* to include regions that are not simple.\n\nLet's suppose that we've proved that Green's theorem is true for type I and type II regions. How can we use this to show that Green's theorem is also true for regions containing holes?\n\nTo illustrate how this is done, let's consider the vector field $\\mathbf F = P\\,\\mathbf i + Q\\,\\mathbf j,$ and the line integral \n\n$$\n\\displaystyle\\oint\\limits_{\\partial D} \\mathbf F\\cdot \\textrm d \\mathbf r\n$$\n\n \n\nwhere $D$ is the annular region below.\n\n![](images/57/57c22c3d7a92ce4a1a1a798f7e6a861ded2e9a6be4d8d98c36869fe29e2783d0.png)\n\nNote that $\\partial D= C_1\\