 geometric random variable, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{SD}[X] = \\sqrt{\\textrm{Var}[X]} = \\dfrac{\\sqrt{1-p}}{p}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for our random variable\n\n$$\nX \\sim \\textrm{Geom}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{3}\\right)\n$$\n\nwe have the following standard deviation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nSD[X] &= \\left(\\sqrt{1 - \\frac{1}{3}}\\right)/\\left(\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\right) \\\\\n&= \\left(\\sqrt{\\frac{2}{3}}\\right)/\\left(\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right)\\right) \\\\\n&= \\sqrt{6}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545747 17592186576411 true] [17592186157210 1074 "We interpret a \"success\" as the event that a $2$ is shown and a \"failure\" when some other number is displayed. So, the success probability is\n\n$$\np=\\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ represent the number of rolls we must do until a 2 is obtained. So, we can model $X$ using a geometric distribution:\n\n$$\nX \\sim \\textrm{Geom}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{8}\\right)\n$$\n\nRecall that, if\n\n$$\nX \\sim\\textrm{Geom}\\left(p\\right)\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\dfrac{1-p}{p^2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for our random variable\n\n$$\nX \\sim \\textrm{Geom}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{8}\\right)\n$$\n\nwe have the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{Var} [X] &= (1 - \\frac{1}{8})/(\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)^{2}) \\\\\n&= (\\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right))/(\\left(\\frac{1}{64}\\right)) \\\\\n&= 56\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545747 17592186576411 false] [17592186157210 1074 "We interpret a \"success\" as the event that a $2$ is shown and a \"failure\" when some other number is displayed. So, the success probability is\n\n$$\np=\\dfrac{1}{8}\n$$\n\nLet $X$ represent the number of rolls we must do until a 2 is obtained. So, we can model $X$ using a geometric distribution:\n\n$$\nX \\sim \\textrm{Geom}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{8}\\right)\n$$\n\nRecall that, if\n\n$$\nX \\sim\\textrm{Geom}\\left(p\\right)\n$$\n\nthen\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}[X] = \\dfrac{1-p}{p^2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for our random variable\n\n$$\nX \\sim \\textrm{Geom}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{8}\\right)\n$$\n\nwe have the following variance:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\operatorname{Var} [X] &= \\left(1 - \\frac{1}{8}\\right)/\\left(\\left(\\frac{1}{8}\\right)^{2}\\right) \\\\\n&= \\left(\\left(\\frac{7}{8}\\right)\\right)/\\left(\\left(\\frac{1}{64}\\right)\\right) \\\\\n&= 56\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545747 17592186576411 true] [17592186157591 1071 "What is $(35 + \\frac{2}{7} + \\frac{5}{49})_{10}$ written in base $7$?" 13194139545747 17592186576411 false] [17592186157591 1071 "What is $\\left(35 + \\frac{2}{7} + \\frac{5}{49}\\right)_{10}$ written in base $7$?" 13194139545747 17592186576411 true] [17592186157592 1071 "What is $(12 + \\frac{2}{3} + \\frac{8}{9})_{10}$ written in base $3$?" 13194139545747 17592186576411 false] [17592186157592 1071 "What is $\\left(12 + \\frac{2}{3} + \\frac{8}{9}\\right)_{10}$ written in base $3$?" 13194139545747 17592186576411 true] [17592186157605 1074 "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the third raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{3}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}(2x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{4}-\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{5})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{3}{10}{x}^{5}-\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{6})|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{3\\cdot {2}^{5}}{10}-\\frac{{2}^{6}}{8})-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{48}{5}-8 \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545747 17592186576411 false] [17592186157605 1074 "For a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nSo, we compute the third raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{3}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{x}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{4}(2x-{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}\\left(\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{4}-\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{5}\\right)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{3}{10}{x}^{5}-\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{6}\\right)|}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{3\\cdot {2}^{5}}{10}-\\frac{{2}^{6}}{8}\\right)-0 \\\\  & =\\frac{48}{5}-8 \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545747 17592186576411 true] [17592186157606 1074 "First, we simplify using the hint and the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[2X-{X}^{3}+1] & =2\\text{E}[X]-\\text{E}[{X}^{3}]+\\text{E}[1] \\\\  & =2\\text{E}[X]-\\text{E}[{X}^{3}]+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe already know $\\text{E}[X].$ So, we compute the third raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{3}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3{x}^{2}+4}{5}\\right)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(\\frac{3}{5}{x}^{5}+\\frac{4}{5}{x}^{3})\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{1}{10}{x}^{6}+\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{4})|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}+\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{11}{20}$ and $\\text{E}[{X}^{3}]=\\frac{3}{10}$ into the simplified expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[2X-{X}^{3}+1] & =2\\text{E}[X]-\\text{E}[{X}^{3}]+1 \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{11}{20}-\\frac{3}{10}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{10}-\\frac{3}{10}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545747 17592186576411 false] [17592186157606 1074 "First, we simplify using the hint and the properties of expected value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[2X-{X}^{3}+1] & =2\\text{E}[X]-\\text{E}[{X}^{3}]+\\text{E}[1] \\\\  & =2\\text{E}[X]-\\text{E}[{X}^{3}]+1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor a continuous random variable $X$ with probability density function $f(x)$ defined over a set $S,$ the $n$th raw moment of $X$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[{X}^{n}]={\\int }_{S}{x}^{n}\\cdot f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe already know $\\text{E}[X].$ So, we compute the third raw moment of $X$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{3}] & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{x}^{3}\\cdot \\left(\\frac{3{x}^{2}+4}{5}\\right)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\left(\\frac{3}{5}{x}^{5}+\\frac{4}{5}{x}^{3}\\right)\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\left(\\frac{1}{10}{x}^{6}+\\frac{1}{5}{x}^{4}\\right)|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}+\\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{10}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\text{E}[X]=\\frac{11}{20}$ and $\\text{E}[{X}^{3}]=\\frac{3}{10}$ into the simplified expression, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[2X-{X}^{3}+1] & =2\\text{E}[X]-\\text{E}[{X}^{3}]+1 \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{11}{20}-\\frac{3}{10}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{10}-\\frac{3}{10}+1 \\\\  & =\\frac{9}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545747 17592186576411 true] [17592186157616 1074 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the annulus between the disks of radii $2$ and $3$ centered at the origin. \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ we obtain $r=2.$\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9,$ we obtain $r=3.$\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}2\\le r\\le 3,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{1}{({x}^{2}+{y}^{2})^{2}}\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{((r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )^{2}+(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{2})^{2}}\\,r\\,\\text{d}r\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{{r}^{4}}\\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{2}^{3}\\frac{1}{{r}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{[-\\frac{1}{2{r}^{2}}]}_{2}^{3}\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }(\\frac{1}{8}-\\frac{1}{18})\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{72}{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{72}[\\theta ]_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{72}\\cdot 2\\pi  \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\pi }{36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545747 17592186576411 false] [17592186157616 1074 "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\text{ }dA={∬}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\text{ }drd\\theta .\n$$\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the annulus between the disks of radii $2$ and $3$ centered at the origin. \n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\\\ y &=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write our curves in polar coordinates.\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=4,$ we obtain $r=2.$\n\n- For ${x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=9,$ we obtain $r=3.$\n\nAs a result, the region can be expressed in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$ as \n\n$$\n\\mathrm{Δ}=\\{(r,\\theta )\\hspace{0.222em}:\\hspace{0.222em}2\\le r\\le 3,\\hspace{0.222em}0\\le \\theta <2\\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}\\frac{