= \\dfrac{14\\pi}{3}.\n$$" 13194139545747 17592186576411 true] [17592186158492 1064 "A **geometric distribution** is a probability distribution that models the total number of independent Bernoulli trials until the first success occurs.\n\nIf $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment, then the number of experiments needed before the first success has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = (1-p)^{x-1}p, \\qquad x=1,2,3,\\ldots\n$$\n\nIf a random variable $X$ follows a geometric distribution with probability $p,$ then we write $X\\sim\\textrm{Geom}(p).$\n\nFor example, suppose we repeatedly flip a coin until we get heads. Each flip of the coin is a binomial experiment, and the probability of success is $p=\\dfrac12.$\n\nLet $X$ be the number of flips needed in order to get heads. Then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle f ( x ) & \\displaystyle  = ( 1 - p {)}^{x - 1} p \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {( 1 - {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} )}^{x - 1} ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} )}^{x - 1} ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} )}^{x}\\end{array}\n$$\n\nIn particular, the probability of needing $3$ tosses until we get heads is \n\n$$\n\\begin{array}{rl}\\displaystyle P ( X = 3 ) & \\displaystyle  = f ( 3 ) \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{2}} )}^{3} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{8}} \\\\ \\displaystyle  & \\displaystyle  = 0.125 .\\end{array}\n$$" 13194139545747 17592186576411 false] [17592186158492 1064 "A **geometric distribution** is a probability distribution that models the total number of independent Bernoulli trials until the first success occurs.\n\nIf $p$ is the probability of success on each experiment, then the number of experiments needed before the firs