,0\\le y\\le 1 \\\\ 0, & \\,\\text{otherwise}\\end{cases}\n$$" 13194139545747 17592186576411 true] [17592186162404 1074 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=x{y}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{3},\\,0\\le y\\le 3\\},$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero.\n\n![](images/68/68da52d856f882614241d72cff4dc3f5947cf16a6c9f58b0aea1c58d7eb375cc.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X{Y}^{3}] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}x{y}^{3}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x{y}^{3}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{3}}x{y}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{9{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{3}y{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{3}}x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{3}y{[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{x=0}^{x={y}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{7}\\text{ }\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{[\\frac{{y}^{8}}{8}]}_{y=0}^{y=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\left(\\frac{{3}^{8}}{8}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{729}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545747 17592186576411 false] [17592186162404 1074 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=x{y}^{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's sketch the region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le {y}^{3},\\,0\\le y\\le 3\\},$ where $f(x,y)$ is nonzero.\n\n![](images/68/68da52d856f882614241d72cff4dc3f5947cf16a6c9f58b0aea1c58d7eb375cc.png)\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X{Y}^{3}] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}x{y}^{3}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}x{y}^{3}\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{3}}x{y}^{3}\\cdot \\frac{2}{9{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{3}y{\\int }_{0}^{{y}^{3}}x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{3}y{\\left[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\right]}_{x=0}^{x={y}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{y}^{7}\\text{ }\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{\\left[\\frac{{y}^{8}}{8}\\right]}_{y=0}^{y=3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\left(\\frac{{3}^{8}}{8}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{729}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545747 17592186576411 true] [17592186162405 1074 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)={x}^{3}(y+1).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonzero only in the rectangular region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 3,\\,1\\le y\\le 2\\}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{3}(Y+1)] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}{x}^{3}(y+1)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{3}(y+1)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{3}(y+1)\\cdot \\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}y(y+1){\\int }_{1}^{3}x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}y(y+1){[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}]}_{1}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}4y(y+1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={[\\frac{4{y}^{3}}{3}+2{y}^{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & ={[\\frac{4{y}^{3}}{3}+2{y}^{2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{32}{3}+8)-(\\frac{4}{3}+2) \\\\  & =\\frac{46}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545747 17592186576411 false] [17592186162405 1074 "Suppose $X$ and $Y$ are continuous random variables, and $f(x,y)$ is their joint probability density function. If $r:{R}^{2}\\to R$ is a function of two variables, then the rule of the lazy statistician states that\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[r(X,Y)]={∬}_{{R}^{2}}r(x,y)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nr(x,y)={x}^{3}(y+1).\n$$\n\nNotice that $f(x,y)$ is nonzero only in the rectangular region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,1\\le x\\le 3,\\,1\\le y\\le 2\\}.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{3}(Y+1)] & ={∬}_{{R}^{2}}{x}^{3}(y+1)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}{x}^{3}(y+1)\\,f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}x\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{3}{x}^{3}(y+1)\\cdot \\frac{y}{{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}y(y+1){\\int }_{1}^{3}x\\,\\text{d}x\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}y(y+1){\\left[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}\\right]}_{1}^{3}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}4y(y+1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\left[\\frac{4{y}^{3}}{3}+2{y}^{2}\\right]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & ={\\left[\\frac{4{y}^{3}}{3}+2{y}^{2}\\right]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{32}{3}+8\\right)-\\left(\\frac{4}{3}+2\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{46}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545747 17592186576411 true] [17592186162412 1071 "Let $X$ and $Y$ be discrete random variables with the joint probability mass function $f(x, y)$ shown below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$f$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y = 0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$Y = 1$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$X = 2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0.15$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\