td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\">$10.67$<br>$4.95$<br>$3.40$<br>$0.32$<br>$0.23$<br>$0.11$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr rowspan=\"3\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$6$</td>\n<td data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$p = 0.010$<br>$p = 0.050$<br>$p = 0.100$<br>$p = 0.900$<br>$p = 0.950$<br>$p = 0.990$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\">$3.4$<br>$4.53$<br>$3.18$<br>$0.25$<br>$0.16$<br>$0.07$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\">$8.75$<br>$4.39$<br>$3.11$<br>$0.29$<br>$0.20$<br>$0.09$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"right\">$8.47$<br>$4.28$<br>$3.05$<br>$0.33$<br>$0.23$<br>$0.12$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nIf $X∼F(6, 5)$, then calculate the following probability.\n\n$P\\left(3.40 \\le X \\le \\frac{1}{0.20}\\right) =$\n{{field-1}}\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." 13194139545747 17592186576411 true] [17592186162534 1071 "Given that the random variable $X∼\\operatorname{Exp} (3)$, calculate $$\n\\displaystyle P(X \\le \\frac{1}{3} \\mid X > \\frac{1}{4}).\n$$" 13194139545747 17592186576411 false] [17592186162534 1071 "Given that the random variable $X∼\\operatorname{Exp} (3)$, calculate $$\n\\displaystyle P\\left(X \\le \\frac{1}{3} \\mid X > \\frac{1}{4}\\right).\n$$" 13194139545747 17592186576411 true] [17592186162610 1074 "We have the linear Diophantine equation\n\n$$\nax+by=c\n$$\n\nwhere $a=6,$ $b=8,$ and $c=10.$ We're given that the equation has solutions.\n\nNotice that our equation can't be solved for either of the two variables using integer coefficients. So, let's find the linear representation of $\\text{gcd}(6,8)$ using the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}8 & = & 6\\cdot 1 & + & 2 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 6 & = & 2\\cdot 3 & + & 0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the remainders (the rightmost terms) in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n2=8-6\\cdot 1.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that $d=\\text{gcd}(a,b)=2$ and\n\n$$\n6\\cdot (-1)+8=2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying this equation by $5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\cdot (-1)+8 & =2 \\\\ 6\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 5+8\\cdot 5 & =10 \\\\ 6\\cdot (-5)+8\\cdot 5 & =10,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that $(x,y)=(-5,5)$ is a solution to our Diophantine equation.\n\nNow, the general solution is\n\n$$\n(-5+\\frac{b}{d}t,\\,5-\\frac{a}{d}t)=(-5+4t,\\,5-3t),\n$$\n\nwhere $t=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…\\,.$\n\nThe greatest possible negative value of $x=-5+4t$ is attained when $t=1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{∼}{x}=-5+4\\cdot 1=-1.\n$$" 13194139545747 17592186576411 false] [17592186162610 1074 "We have the linear Diophantine equation\n\n$$\nax+by=c\n$$\n\nwhere $a=6,$ $b=8,$ and $c=10.$ We're given that the equation has solutions.\n\nNotice that our equation can't be solved for either of the two variables using integer coefficients. So, let's find the linear representation of $\\text{gcd}(6,8)$ using the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}8 & = & 6\\cdot 1 & + & 2 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 6 & = & 2\\cdot 3 & + & 0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the remainders (the rightmost terms) in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n2=8-6\\cdot 1.\n$$\n\nAs a result, we obtain that $d=\\text{gcd}(a,b)=2$ and\n\n$$\n6\\cdot (-1)+8=2.\n$$\n\nMultiplying this equation by $5,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6\\cdot (-1)+8 & =2 \\\\ 6\\cdot (-1)\\cdot 5+8\\cdot 5 & =10 \\\\ 6\\cdot (-5)+8\\cdot 5 & =10,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich means that $(x,y)=(-5,5)$ is a solution to our Diophantine equation.\n\nNow, the general solution is\n\n$$\n\\left(-5+\\frac{b}{d}t,\\,5-\\frac{a}{d}t\\right)=(-5+4t,\\,5-3t),\n$$\n\nwhere $t=0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,…\\,.$\n\nThe greatest possible negative value of $x=-5+4t$ is attained when $t=1.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\overset{∼}{x}=-5+4\\cdot 1=-1.\n$$" 13194139545747 17592186576411 true] [17592186162641 1074 "To find the interquartile range, we first need to find the lower quartile and the upper quartile.\n\nThe lower quartile of a random variable $X$ is the number ${q}_{1}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {q}_{1})=\\frac{1}{4},\n$$\n\nand the upper quartile is the number ${q}_{3}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {q}_{3})=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the lower quartile:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {q}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ F({q}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 1-{e}^{1-{q}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {e}^{1-{q}_{1}} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 1-{q}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right) \\\\ {q}_{1} & =1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the upper quartile:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {q}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ F({q}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 1-{e}^{1-{q}_{3}} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {e}^{1-{q}_{3}} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 1-{q}_{3} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right) \\\\ {q}_{3} & =1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the interquartile range is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}IQR & ={q}_{3}-{q}_{1} \\\\  & =1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)-(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545747 17592186576411 false] [17592186162641 1074 "To find the interquartile range, we first need to find the lower quartile and the upper quartile.\n\nThe lower quartile of a random variable $X$ is the number ${q}_{1}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {q}_{1})=\\frac{1}{4},\n$$\n\nand the upper quartile is the number ${q}_{3}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(X\\le {q}_{3})=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nFirst, we find the lower quartile:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {q}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ F({q}_{1}) & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 1-{e}^{1-{q}_{1}} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ {e}^{1-{q}_{1}} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 1-{q}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right) \\\\ {q}_{1} & =1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the upper quartile:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le {q}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ F({q}_{3}) & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ 1-{e}^{1-{q}_{3}} & =\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ {e}^{1-{q}_{3}} & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ 1-{q}_{3} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right) \\\\ {q}_{3} & =1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the interquartile range is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}IQR & ={q}_{3}-{q}_{1} \\\\  & =1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right)-\\left(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{3}{4}\\right)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡\\left(\\frac{1}{4}\\right) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545747 17592186576411 true] [17592186162685 1071 "Given that the random variable $X∼\\text{Exp}(2),$ calculate $P(X<\\frac{1}{6}).$ Round your final answer to four decimal places." 13194139545747 17592186576411 false] [17592186162685 1071 "Given that the random variable $X∼\\text{Exp}(2),$ calculate $P\\left(X<\\frac{1}{6}\\right).$ Round your final answer to four decimal places." 13194139545747 17592186576411 true] [17592186162685 1074 "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(2).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-2x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P(X<\\frac{1}{6})$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<\\frac{1}{6}) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/6}2{e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2x}{|}_{0}^{1/6} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-1/3}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.2835\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545747 17592186576411 false] [17592186162685 1074 "An exponential random variable $X$ with rate $\\lambda $ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\lambda {e}^{-\\lambda x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nWe're given that\n\n$$\nX∼\\text{Exp}(2).\n$$\n\nSo, $X$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{e}^{-2x}, & x\\ge 0 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can calculate $P\\left(X<\\frac{1}{6}\\right)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P\\left(X<\\frac{1}{6}\\right) & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/6}2{e}^{-2x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{e}^{-2x}{|}_{0}^{1/6} \\\\  & =-{e}^{-1/3}+{e}^{0} \\\\  & ≈0.2835\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545747 17592186576411 true] [17592186163177 1064 "**The chi-square distribution** with $k$ **degrees of freedom**, denoted $\\chi^2(k)$, is the distribution of a random variable that is the sum of the squares of $k$ independent standard normal random variables. \n\nThus, if $Z_1, Z_2, \\ldots, Z_k$ are mutually independent, where $Z_i\\sim N(0,1)$, then\n\n$$\nZ_1^2+Z_2^2+\\ldots+Z_k^2\\sim  \\chi^2(k).\n$$\n\nIt can be shown that $X \\sim \\chi^2(k)$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{ {2}^{k / 2} \\mathrm{\\Gamma } ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{k}{2}} )}} {x}^{k / 2 - 1} {e}^{- x / 2} , & x \\ge  0 \\\\ 0 , & \\text{otherwise}\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nNote that $\\Gamma$ is the gamma function.\n\nThe diagram below shows $f(x)$ for various degrees of freedom $k.$\n\n![](images/ce/cecc3fc82e618f5b5f91e61c4dcab6c1d38b970af20f0ae2baf929b0ce9cdbb3.png)\n\nThe chi-square distribution is widely used in inferential statistics. In particular, it is used in **goodness of fit** tests, where experimental data is compared against a hypothesized theoretical model. You will learn more about the applications of the chi-square distribution in future lessons." 13194139545747 17592186576411 false] [17592186163177 1064 "**The chi-square distribution** with $k$ **degrees of freedom**, denoted $\\chi^2(k)$, is the distribution of a random variable that is the sum of the squares of $k$ independent standard normal random variables. \n\nThus, if $Z_1, Z_2, \\ldots, Z_k$ are mutually independent, where $Z_i\\sim N(0,1)$, then\n\n$$\nZ_1^2+Z_2^2+\\ldots+Z_k^2\\sim  \\chi^2(k).\n$$\n\nIt can be shown that $X \\sim \\chi^2(k)$ has the following probability density function:\n\n$$\nf(x) = {\\left\\{  \\begin{array}{ll}{\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{ {2}^{k / 2} \\mathrm{\\Gamma } \\left( {\\displaystyle \\frac{k}{2}} \\right)}} {x}^{k / 2 - 1} {e}^{- x / 2} , & x \\ge  0 \\\\ 0 , & \\text{otherwise}\\end{array} \\right. }\n$$\n\nNote that $\\Gamma$ is the gamma function.\n\nThe diagram below shows $f(x)$ for various degrees of freedom $k.$\n\n![](images/ce/cecc3fc82e618f5b5f91e61c4dcab6c1d38b970af20f0ae2baf929b0ce9cdbb3.png)\n\nThe chi-square distribution is widely used in inferential statistics. In particular, it is used in **goodness of fit** tests, where experimental data is compared against a hypothesized theoretical model. You will learn more about the applications of the chi-square distribution in future lessons." 13194139545747 17592186576411 true] [17592186163185 1064 "Given that the random variable $X\\sim \\textrm{Exp}(2),$ let's calculate the following probability:\n\n$$\nP\\left(X > \\dfrac32 \\,\\bigg|\\, X > \\dfrac12\\right)\n$$\n\nGiven a random variable $X \\sim \\mathrm{Exp}(\\lambda)$ and two positive real numbers $s, t,$ the memoryless property of the exponential distribution states that\n\n$$\nP(X>s+t \\, \\big|\\, X>s) = P(X > t) = e^{-\\lambda t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have \n\n$$\n\\lambda = 2, \\qquad s=\\dfrac 12,\\qquad s+t=\\dfrac32.\n$$\n\nFrom this, we can find $t$ as follows:\n\n$$\nt = (t+s) - s = \\dfrac32-\\dfrac12 = 1\n$$\n\nSubstituting these values into the above formula, we 