t become horizontal as $y\\to \\pm \\infty .$\n\n- The equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{3}{y-1}$ does *not* give the required behavior. The slopes of the slope field for this differential equation are positive for $y>1.$" 13194139545747 17592186576411 true] [17592186167094 1074 "Since our ellipse is centered at $(2,-1)$ and has semiaxes $3$ and $4,$ its equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-(-1))^{2}}{{4}^{2}}=1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16}) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16}\\right) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2(x-2)}{9}+\\frac{2(y+1)}{16}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{y+1}{8}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2(x-2)}{9} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{16(x-2)}{9(y+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545747 17592186576411 false] [17592186167094 1074 "Since our ellipse is centered at $(2,-1)$ and has semiaxes $3$ and $4,$ its equation is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{{3}^{2}}+\\frac{(y-(-1))^{2}}{{4}^{2}}=1\\,⟹\\,\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16}=1.\n$$\n\nNow, to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x},$ we use implicit differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9}+\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16}\\right) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(1) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9}\\right)+\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}\\left(\\frac{(y+1)^{2}}{16}\\right) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2(x-2)}{9}+\\frac{2(y+1)}{16}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{y+1}{8}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{2(x-2)}{9} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-\\frac{16(x-2)}{9(y+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545747 17592186576411 true] [17592186167117 1074 "Let $F$ be the event that a student is female, $B$ be the event that a student takes biology, and ${C}_{1},{C}_{2},{C}_{3}$ be the event that a student is in class $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({C}_{1})=0.25 &  & P(B|{C}_{1})=0.55 \\\\  & P({C}_{2})=0.35 &  & P(B|{C}_{2})=0.4 \\\\  & P({C}_{3})=0.4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${C}_{1}⊔{C}_{2}⊔{C}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({C}_{1})+P({C}_{2})+P({C}_{3})=0.25+0.35+0.4=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $45\\%$ of students in the entire school are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F)=0.45.\n$$\n\n- Since $80\\%$ of females take biology, we have\n\n$$\nP(B|F)=0.8.\n$$\n\n- Since $70\\%$ of biology students are female, we have\n\n$$\nP(F|B)=0.7.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen student from class $3$ takes biology, which is represented as $P(B|{C}_{3}).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(F|B) & =\\frac{P(B|F)P(F)}{P(B)} \\\\ P(F|B) & =\\frac{P(B|F)P(F)}{P(B|{C}_{1})P({C}_{1})+P(B|{C}_{2})P({C}_{2})+P(B|{C}_{3})P({C}_{3})} \\\\ 0.7 & =\\frac{(0.8)(0.45)}{(0.55)(0.25)+(0.4)(0.35)+P(B|{C}_{3})(0.4)} \\\\ 0.7 & =\\frac{0.36}{0.2775+0.4P(B|{C}_{3})}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(B|{C}_{3}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.7(0.2775+0.4P(B|{C}_{3})) & =0.36 \\\\ 0.2775+0.4P(B|{C}_{3}) & =\\frac{0.36}{0.7} \\\\ 0.2775+0.4P(B|{C}_{3}) & =\\frac{18}{35} \\\\ 0.4P(B|{C}_{3}) & =\\frac{18}{35}-0.2775 \\\\ P(B|{C}_{3}) & =\\frac{1}{0.4}(\\frac{18}{35}-0.2775) \\\\ P(B|{C}_{3}) & ≈0.592\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139545747 17592186576411 false] [17592186167117 1074 "Let $F$ be the event that a student is female, $B$ be the event that a student takes bi