et's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From the bullet points, we have the following information:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & P({M}_{1})=0.45 &  & P(G|{M}_{1})=0.8 \\\\  & P({M}_{2})=0.35 &  & P(G|{M}_{2}) \\\\  & P({M}_{3})=0.2 &  & P(G|{M}_{3})=0.9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe disjoint union ${M}_{1}⊔{M}_{2}⊔{M}_{3}$ covers the entire sample space, and we have\n\n$$\nP({M}_{1})+P({M}_{2})+P({M}_{3})=0.45+0.35+0.2=1.\n$$\n\n- Since $65\\%$ of muffins are made with yeast A, we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.65.\n$$\n\n- Since $82\\%$ of muffins made with yeast A turned out well, we have\n\n$$\nP(G|A)=0.82.\n$$\n\n- Since $68\\%$ of good muffins were prepared with yeast A, we have\n\n$$\nP(A|G)=0.68.\n$$\n\nWe want to find the probability that a randomly chosen muffin cooked with mode $2$ turned well, which is represented as $P(G|{M}_{2}).$ \n\nUsing Bayes' theorem combined with the law of total probability, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(A|G) & =\\frac{P(G|A)P(A)}{P(G)} \\\\ P(A|G) & =\\frac{P(G|A)P(A)}{P(G|{M}_{1})P({M}_{1})+P(G|{M}_{2})P({M}_{2})+P(G|{M}_{3})P({M}_{3})} \\\\ 0.68 & =\\frac{(0.82)(0.65)}{(0.8)(0.45)+P(G|{M}_{2})(0.35)+(0.9)(0.2)} \\\\ 0.68 & =\\frac{0.533}{0.35P(G|{M}_{2})+0.54}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $P(G|{M}_{2}),$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.68(0.35P(G|{M}_{2})+0.54) & =0.533 \\\\ 0.35P(G|{M}_{2})+0.54 & =\\frac{0.533}{0.68} \\\\ 0.35P(G|{M}_{2})+0.54 & =\\frac{533}{680} \\\\ 0.35P(G|{M}_{2}) & =\\frac{533}{680}-0.54 \\\\ P(G|{M}_{2}) & =\\frac{1}{0.35}(\\frac{533}{680}-0.54) \\\\ P(G|{M}_{2}) & ≈0.697\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139545747 17592186576411 false] [17592186167119 1074 "Let $A$ be the event that a muffin is made with yeast A, $G$ be the event that a muffin turns out well, and ${M}_{1},{M}_{2},{M}_{3}$ be the event that a muffin is cooked with mode $1,$ $2,$ or $3,$ respectively. \n\nLet's break down the information given in the problem statement:\n\n- From th