 we calculate ${T}^{′}(t)$ and its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{T}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{t}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}\\left(\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}\\right),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(0)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{4}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}},\\,-\\frac{2t}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}},\\,0⟩ \\\\ ‖{T}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{(4)^{2}+{(-2t)}^{2}+{(0)}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{16+4{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}+4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we normalize ${T}^{′}(t)$ and obtain the principal normal to the curve:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}N(t) & =\\frac{{T}^{′}(t)}{‖{T}^{′}(t)‖}=\\frac{\\frac{1}{({t}^{2}+4)^{3/2}}⟨4,\\,-2t,\\,0⟩}{\\left(\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}+4}\\right)}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}⟨2,\\,-t,\\,0⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the binormal vector, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(t) & =T(t)\\times N(t) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}⟨t,2,0⟩\\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{{t}^{2}+4}}⟨2,\\,-t,\\,0⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ t & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -t & 0\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+4}\\left[\\text{ }\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -t & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,i-\\begin{vmatrix}t & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{vmatrix}\\,j+\\begin{vmatrix}t & 2 \\\\ 2 & -t\\end{vmatrix}\\,k\\text{ }\\right] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+4}⟨0+0,-(0-0),-{t}^{2}-4⟩ \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{t}^{2}+4}⟨0,\\,0,\\,-({t}^{2}+4)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨0,0,-1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139545747 17592186576411 false] [17592186167989 1064 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 3x - 2y + 5, \\qquad y(0) = 1\n$$\n\nOur goal is to approximate $y(1)$ using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method.\n\nSince we know $y(0)$ and wish to estimate $y(1),$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 1 - 0 = 1.\n$$\n\nLet's use a table to keep track of all important values, including the intermediate slopes. We start by adding the initial condition data $y(0)=1.$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_1\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_2\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_3\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNow let's find the required values.\n\n- First, we compute the value of $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} & =3x-2y+5 \\\\  & =3\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 1+5 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_1\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_2\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_3\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- The first intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,({x}_{1},{y}_{1}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{'}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1,\\,1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{5}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{'} & =3{x}_{1}-2{y}_{1}+5 \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}+5 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_1\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_2\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_3\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac32$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- The second intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,({x}_{2},{y}_{2}) & =(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{'}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1,\\,1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{7}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{'} & =3{x}_{2}-2{y}_{2}+5 \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{7}{4}+5 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_1\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_2\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_3\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac32$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- The third intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{3},{y}_{3}) & =(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{'}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(0+1,\\,1+3\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(1,4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the third intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{3}^{'} & =3{x}_{3}-2{y}_{3}+5 \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 4+5 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_1\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_2\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_3\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac32$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ using the Runge-Kutta Method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{'}+2{y}_{1}^{'}+2{y}_{2}^{'}+{y}_{3}^{'})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(3+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+2\\cdot 3+0)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed first row is as given below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_1\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_2\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_3\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac32$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x$ and $\\Delta y$ to $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =1+2 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_1\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_2\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_3\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac32$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLooking at the two leftmost columns, we conclude that $y(1) \\approx 3.$" 13194139545747 17592186576411 false] [17592186167989 1064 "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\ny' = 3x - 2y + 5, \\qquad y(0) = 1\n$$\n\nOur goal is to approximate $y(1)$ using the Runge-Kutta (RK4) method.\n\nSince we know $y(0)$ and wish to estimate $y(1),$ we will use a step size of\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = 1 - 0 = 1.\n$$\n\nLet's use a table to keep track of all important values, including the intermediate slopes. We start by adding the initial condition data $y(0)=1.$\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_1\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_2\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_3\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nNow let's find the required values.\n\n- First, we compute the value of $y'$ according to the given rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{'} & =3x-2y+5 \\\\  & =3\\cdot 0-2\\cdot 1+5 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we add this to our table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_1\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_2\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_3\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- The first intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,({x}_{1},{y}_{1}) & =\\left(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{'}\\cdot Δx\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1,\\,1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 3\\cdot 1\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{5}{2}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{'} & =3{x}_{1}-2{y}_{1}+5 \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{5}{2}+5 \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_1\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_2\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_3\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac32$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- The second intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,({x}_{2},{y}_{2}) & =\\left(x+\\frac{1}{2}Δx,\\,y+\\frac{1}{2}{y}_{1}^{'}\\cdot Δx\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(0+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 1,\\,1+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 1\\right) \\\\  & =\\left(\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{7}{4}\\right).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{'} & =3{x}_{2}-2{y}_{2}+5 \\\\  & =3\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}-2\\cdot \\frac{7}{4}+5 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe add this to our table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_1\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_2\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_3\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac32$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\n- The third intermediate point is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({x}_{3},{y}_{3}) & =(x+Δx,\\,y+{y}_{2}^{'}\\cdot Δx) \\\\  & =(0+1,\\,1+3\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =(1,4).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the third intermediate slope is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{3}^{'} & =3{x}_{3}-2{y}_{3}+5 \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1-2\\cdot 4+5 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can add this to our table.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_1\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_2\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_3\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac32$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nFinally, we compute $\\Delta y$ using the Runge-Kutta Method:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δy & =\\frac{1}{6}({y}^{'}+2{y}_{1}^{'}+2{y}_{2}^{'}+{y}_{3}^{'})\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\left(3+2\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}+2\\cdot 3+0\\right)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the completed first row is as given below.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_1\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_2\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_3\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac32$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nTo get the values of $x$ and $y$ in the *next* row, we add $\\Delta x$ to $x$ and $\\Delta y$ to $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{new}} & =x+Δx \\\\  & =0+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ {y}_{\\text{new}} & =y+Δy \\\\  & =1+2 \\\\  & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding these rows to our table gives the following:\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,x\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_1\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_2\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,y'_3\\,$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\Delta y$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr data-next-row=\"true\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$\\dfrac32$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$0$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table></div>\n\n\nLooking at the two leftmost columns, we conclude that $y(1) \\approx 3.$" 13194139545747 17592186576411 true] [17592186168016 1064 "Recall that the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} &=ax(1-hx)-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′} &=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nhas equilibria at\n\n$$\n(0,0), \\qquad\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{h}, 0\\right), \\qquad\n\\left(\\dfrac{c}{d}, \\dfrac{a}{b}\\left(1 - \\dfrac{c}{d}h\\right)\\right).\n$$\n\nNotice that the second component of the third equilibrium point is positive only when \n\n$$\nh < \\dfrac{d}{c},\n$$\n\nwhich guarantees that the third fixed point will lie in the first quadrant. For now, let's consider only this case.\n\nWe now evaluate the Jacobian matrix\n$$\nJ\n=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}a ( 1 - 2 h x ) - b y & - b x \\\\ d y & - c + d x\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\nat the nonzero equilibrium $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 / h \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}$ to get\n\n$$\nJ(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast)\n=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}a ( 1 - 2 h ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{h}} ) ) - b ( 0 ) & - b ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{h}} ) \\\\ d ( 0 ) & - c + d ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{h}} )\\end{array} \\right]}\n=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}- a & - {\\displaystyle \\frac{b}{h}} \\\\ 0 & {\\displaystyle \\frac{c}{h}} ( {\\displaystyle \\frac{d}{c}} - h )\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nBecause we are considering the case when $h < \\dfrac{d}{c},$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{c}{h}\\left(\\dfrac{d}{c}-h\\right) > 0.\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are $\\lambda_1 = -a < 0$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\lambda_2 = \\dfrac{c}{h}\\left(\\dfrac{d}{c}-h\\right) > 0.\n$$ Since eigenvalues are real with opposite signs, our equilibrium is an *unstable saddle*.\n\nAnalyzing the type of the third equilibrium in full generality becomes considerably more cumbersome. Therefore, we'll examine a concrete example later in the lesson. For now, we simply note that the third equilibrium is an *asymptotically stable (spiral) sink*.\n\nA typical phase portrait of the system, in the case when $h < \\dfrac{d}{c},$ looks as shown below.\n\n![](images/8c/8c82332ed5277586a2b97e900383619a5d61cdb73ff4b60a92de3d5460165165.png)\n\nThe global picture is the following:\n\n- There are two straight-line types of solutions:\n\n- Along the $x$-axis, trajectories approach the equilibrium at $\\left(\\dfrac{1}{h},0\\right).$ Indeed, in the absence of the predators, the prey approaches the carrying capacity.\n\n- Along the $y$-axis, trajectories approach the origin. Indeed, in the absence of the prey, the predators decrease exponentially.\n\n- The curved trajectories converge to the equilibrium point $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\dfrac{c}{d}, \\dfrac{a}{b}\\left(1 - \\dfrac{c}{d}h\\right)\\right).\n$$\n\nThe corresponding population dynamics graphs look as depicted below.\n\n![](images/a7/a7673170825344e1831f2ba01ce4c20f10091380ff4e116f3b6bd9e7666e51aa.png)" 13194139545747 17592186576411 false] [17592186168016 1064 "Recall that the system\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′} &=ax(1-hx)-bxy \\\\ {y}^{′} &=-cy+dxy\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nhas equilibria at\n\n$$\n(0,0), \\qquad\n\\left(\\dfrac{1}{h}, 0\\right), \\qquad\n\\left(\\dfrac{c}{d}, \\dfrac{a}{b}\\left(1 - \\dfrac{c}{d}h\\right)\\right).\n$$\n\nNotice that the second component of the third equilibrium point is positive only when \n\n$$\nh < \\dfrac{d}{c},\n$$\n\nwhich guarantees that the third fixed point will lie in the first quadrant. For now, let's consider only this case.\n\nWe now evaluate the Jacobian matrix\n$$\nJ\n=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}a ( 1 - 2 h x ) - b y & - b x \\\\ d y & - c + d x\\end{array} \\right]}\n$$\nat the nonzero equilibrium $\\mathbf{x}^\\ast = {\\left[ \\begin{array}{c}1 / h \\\\ 0\\end{array} \\right]}$ to get\n\n$$\nJ(\\mathbf{x}^\\ast)\n=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}a \\left( 1 - 2 h \\left( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{h}} \\right) \\right) - b ( 0 ) & - b \\left( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{h}} \\right) \\\\ d ( 0 ) & - c + d \\left( {\\displaystyle \\frac{1}{h}} \\right)\\end{array} \\right]}\n=\n{\\left[ \\begin{array}{cc}- a & - {\\displaystyle \\frac{b}{h}} \\\\ 0 & {\\displaystyle \\frac{c}{h}} \\left( {\\displaystyle \\frac{d}{c}} - h \\right)\\end{array} \\right]}.\n$$\n\nBecause we are considering the case when $h < \\dfrac{d}{c},$ we have that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{c}{h}\\left(\\dfrac{d}{c}-h\\right) > 0.\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the Jacobian matrix are $\\lambda_1 = -a < 0$ and $$\n\\displaystyle \\lambda_2 = \\dfrac{c}{h}\\left(\\dfrac{d}{c}-h\\right) > 0.\n$$ Since eigenvalues are real with opposite signs, our equilibrium is an *unstable saddle*.\n\nAnalyzing the type of the third equilibrium in full generality becomes considerably more cumbersome. Therefore, we'll examine a concrete example later in the lesson. For now, we simply note that the third equilibrium is an *asymptotically stable (spiral) sink*.\n\nA typical phase portrait of the system, in the case when $h < \\dfrac{d}{c},$ looks as shown below.\n\n![](images/8c/8c82332ed5277586a2b97e900383619a5d61cdb73ff4b60a92de3d5460165165.png)\n\nThe global picture is the following:\n\n- There are two straight-line types of solutions:\n\n- Along the $x$-axis, trajectories approach the equilibrium at $\\left(\\dfrac{1}{h},0\\right).$ Indeed, in the absence of the predators, the prey approaches the carrying capacity.\n\n- Along the $y$-axis, trajectories approach the origin. Indeed, in the absence of the prey, the predators decrease exponentially.\n\n- The curved trajectories converge to the equilibrium point $$\n\\displaystyle \\left(\\dfrac{c}{d}, \\dfrac{a}{b}\\left(1 - \\dfrac{c}{d}h\\right)\\right).\n$$\n\nThe corresponding population dynamics graphs look as depicted below.\n\n![](images/a7/a7673170825344e1831f2ba01ce4c20f10091380ff4e116f3b6bd9e7666e51aa.png)" 13194139545747 17592186576411 true] [17592186168027 1064 "Next, we evaluate the Jacobian matrix\n$$\nJ\n=\n{\