}{S_p \\sqrt{\\dfrac{1}{n_x} + \\dfrac{1}{n_y}}}\n$$\n\nfollows a student's $t$-distribution $T_\\nu$ with\n\n$$\n\\nu=n_x+n_y-2\n$$\n\ndegrees of freedom, where\n\n- $\\overline{X}$ and $\\overline{Y}$ are the sample means,\n- $\\mu_x$ and $\\mu_y$ are the population means,\n- $n_x$ and $n_y$ are the sample sizes,\n- $S_x^2$ and $S_y^2$ are the sample variance, and\n- $S_p^2 = \\dfrac{(n_x-1)S_x^2+(n_y-1)S_y^2}{n_x+n_y-2}$ is the pooled estimate of the variance.\n\nIn our example, the null and alternative hypotheses are the following:\n\n- $H_0: \\mu_x = \\mu_y$ is the null hypothesis\n- $H_1: \\mu_x \\gt \\mu_y$ is the alternative (one-tailed) hypothesis\n\nFirst, let's compute the number of degrees of freedom and the pooled estimate of the variance for our samples:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nν &= 16 + 18 - 2 = 32 \\\\\nS_{p}^{2} &= ((16 - 1)(1.75)^{2} + (18 - 1)(2.25)^{2})/(16 + 18 - 2) = \\frac{33}{8}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAssuming the null hypothesis, i.e.,\n\n$$\n\\mu_x-\\mu_y=0\n$$\n\nwe compute the test statistic:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nt &= (x^(―) - y^(―) - (μ_{x} - μ_{y}))/(S_{p}\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{n_{x}} + \\frac{1}{n_{y}}}) \\\\\n&= (15.2 - 13.5 - (0))/(\\sqrt{\\frac{33}{8}} \\cdot \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{16} + \\frac{1}{18}}) \\\\\n&\\approx 2.436\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we determine the critical region corresponding to our significance level.\n\nGiven a random variable $T$ that has a student's $t$-distribution $T_k$ with $k$ degrees of freedom, the table below shows the $t$-scores $t_{k,p}$ such that\n\n$$\nP(T > t_{k,p}) = p\n$$\n\nfor some particular values of $k$ and $p$.\n\n\n\n<div class=\"source-table-scroll\"><table class=\"source-table\" data-rules=\"all\" data-table-align=\"center\" data-frame=\"true\">\n<tbody><tr data-next-row=\"true\" data-cell-align=\"center\">\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$k \\setminus p$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=\"#D6EAF8\">$0.100$</td>\n<td data-cell-align=\"center\" data-fill=