inomial random variable with distribution $B(n, p)$. Now, since any given distribution is characterized by its moment-generating function (meaning that no other distribution can have the same moment-generating function), we conclude that\n$Y\\sim B(n,p)$.\n- **Sums of Binomial Random Variables**: Suppose $X_1\\sim B(n_1,p)$ and $X_2\\sim B(n_2,p)$ are independent. Then\n$Y = X_1+X_2\\sim B(n_1+n_2, p)$.\nTo prove this, let $M_{X_1}(t)$ and $M_{X_2}(t)$ be the moment-generating functions of $X_1$ and $X_2$ respectively. Then, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle M_{X_1}(t) = (pe^t+q)^{n_1}, \\qquad M_{X_2}(t) = (pe^t+q)^{n_2}.\n$$\nBy the multiplicative property of moment-generating functions, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{Y}(t) & ={M}_{{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}}(t) \\\\  & ={M}_{{X}_{1}}(t)\\cdot {M}_{{X}_{2}}(t) \\\\  & =(p{e}^{t}+q)^{{n}_{1}}\\cdot (p{e}^{t}+q)^{{n}_{2}} \\\\  & =(p{e}^{t}+q)^{{n}_{1}+{n}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis is the moment-generating function of a binomial random variable with distribution $B(n_1+n_2, p)$. Therefore, by the uniqueness property, we conclude\n$Y\\sim B(n_1+n_2, p)$.\n- **Sums of Poisson Random Variables**: If $X_1\\sim \\textrm{Po}(\\lambda_1)$ and $X_2\\sim \\textrm{Po}(\\lambda_2)$ are independent, then\n$Y = X_1+X_2\\sim \\textrm{Po}(\\lambda_1+\\lambda_2)$.\nTo prove this, let $M_{X_1}(t)$ and $M_{X_2}(t)$ be the moment-generating functions of $X_1$ and $X_2$ respectively. Then, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle M_{X_1}(t) = e^{\\lambda_1(e^t-1)}, \\qquad M_{X_2}(t) = e^{\\lambda_2(e^t-1)}.\n$$\nBy the multiplicative property of moment-generating functions, we have\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{Y}(t) & ={M}_{{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}}(t) \\\\  & ={M}_{{X}_{1}}(t)\\cdot {M}_{{X}_{2}}(t) \\\\  & ={e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}({e}^{t}-1)}\\cdot {e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}({e}^{t}-1)} \\\\  & ={e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}({e}^{t}-1)+{\\lambda }_{2}({e}^{t}-1)} \\\\  & ={e}^{({\\lambda }_{1}+{\\lambda }_{2})({e}^{t}-1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis is the moment-generating function of a Poisson random variable with distribution $\\textrm{Po}(\\lambda_1+\\lambda_2)$. Therefore, by the uniqueness property, we conclude\n$Y\\sim \\textrm{Po}(\\lambda_1+\\lambda_2)$.\n\n- **Sums of Normal Random Variables**: Suppose $X_1\\sim N(\\mu_1,\\sigma_1^2)$ and $X_2\\sim N(\\mu_2,\\sigma_2^2)$ are independent. Then\n$Y = X_1+X_2\\sim N(\\mu_1+\\mu_2, \\sigma_1^2+\\sigma_2^2)$.\nTo prove this, let $M_{X_1}(t)$ and $M_{X_2}(t)$ be the moment-generating functions of $X_1$ and $X_2$ respectively. Then, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle M_{X_1}(t) = \\exp\\left(\\mu_1 t + \\frac{1}{2} \\sigma^2_1 t^2\\right), \\qquad M_{X_2}(t) = \\exp\\left(\\mu_2 t + \\frac{1}{2} \\sigma^2_2 t^2\\right),\n$$\nwhere $\\exp(x) = e^x$ is the exponential function.By the multiplicative property of moment-generating functions and by the properties of the exponential function, we have that\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{Y}(t) & ={M}_{{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}}(t) \\\\  & ={M}_{{X}_{1}}(t)\\cdot {M}_{{X}_{2}}(t) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{exp}({\\mu }_{1}t+\\frac{1}{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}{t}^{2})\\cdot \\operatorname{exp}({\\mu }_{2}t+\\frac{1}{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{exp}({\\mu }_{1}t+\\frac{1}{2}{\\sigma }_{1}^{2}{t}^{2}+{\\mu }_{2}t+\\frac{1}{2}{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{exp}(({\\mu }_{1}+{\\mu }_{2})t+\\frac{1}{2}({\\sigma }_{1}^{2}+{\\sigma }_{2}^{2}){t}^{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis is the moment-generating function of a normal random variable with distribution $N(\\mu_1+\\mu_2, \\sigma_1^2+\\sigma_2^2)$. Therefore, by the uniqueness property, we conclude\n$Y\\sim N(\\mu_1+\\mu_2, \\sigma_1^2+\\sigma_2^2)$.\n\n- **Sums of Chi-Square Random Variables**:\nSuppose $X_1\\sim \\chi^2(k_1)$ and $X_2\\sim \\chi^2(k_2)$ are independent. Then\n$Y = X_1+X_2\\sim \\chi^2(k_1+k_2)$.\nTo prove this, let $M_{X_1}(t)$ and $M_{X_2}(t)$ be the moment-generating functions of $X_1$ and $X_2$ respectively. Then, we have\n$$\n\\displaystyle M_{X_1}(t) = (1-2t)^{-k_1/2}, \\qquad M_{X_2}(t) = (1-2t)^{-k_2/2}.\n$$\nBy the multiplicative property of moment-generating functions, we have that\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{Y}(t) & ={M}_{{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}}(t) \\\\  & ={M}_{{X}_{1}}(t)\\cdot {M}_{{X}_{2}}(t) \\\\  & =(1-2t)^{-{k}_{1}/2}\\cdot (1-2t)^{-{k}_{2}/2} \\\\  & =(1-2t)^{-({k}_{1}+{k}_{2})/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThis is the moment-generating function of a chi-square random variable with distribution $\\chi^2(k_1+k_2)$. Therefore, by the uniqueness property, we conclude\n$Y\\sim \\chi^2(k_1+k_2)$." 13194139545747 17592186576411 false] [17592186173137 1064 "Let's use our knowledge of the uniqueness property of moment-generating functions to prove some general results.\n\n- **Sums of Bernoulli Random Variables**: Suppose $X_1, X_2, \\ldots, X_n\\sim \\textrm{Bernoulli}(p)$ are independent. Then\n$Y = X_1+X_2+ \\cdots + X_n \\sim B(n,p)$.\nTo prove this, let $M_{X_i}(t)$ be the moment-generating function of $X_i$ for $i=1,2,\\ldots, n$. Then, we have\n$M_{X_i}(t) = 1 - p + pe^t$.\nBy the multiplicative property of moment-generating functions, we have that\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{M}_{Y}(t) & ={M}_{{X}_{1}+{X}_{2}+⋯+{X}_{n}}(t) \\\\  & ={M}_{{X}_{1}}(t)\\cdot {M}_{{X}_{2}}(t)\\cdot \\hspace{0.222em}⋯\\hspace{0.222em}\\cdot {M}_{{X}_{n}}(t) \\\\  & =(1-p+p{e}^{t})\\cdot (1-p+p{e}^{t})\\cdot \\hspace{0.222em}⋯\\hspace{0.222em}\\cdot (1-p+p{e}^{t}) \\\\  & =(1-p+p{e}^{t})^{n},\\end{aligned}\n$$\nwhich is the moment-generating function of a binomial random variable with distribution $B(n, p)$. Now, since any given distribution is characterized by its moment-generating function (meaning that no other distribution can have the same moment-generating function), we conclude that\n$Y\\sim B(n,p)$.\n- **Sums of Binomial Random Variables**: Suppose $X_1\\sim B(n_1,p)$ and $X_2\\sim B(n_2,p)$ are independent. Then\n$Y = X_1+X_2\\sim B(n_1+n_2, p)$.\nTo prove this, let $M_{X_1}(t)$ and $M_{X_2}(t)$ be the moment-generating functions of $X_1$ and $X_2$ respectively. 